      "message": "Wish there was something other than -1 but still made other reviewers aware some discussion needs to happen, anyway:\n\n## Issues:\n\n### Breaking API changes\npatch still breaks existing self-scheduling scripts that delete assignments because it removes the ability to send the assignment ID in a JSON payload.\n\n## Good stuff:\nNice foundation - username is source of truth and fundamentals are all there.\nReally nice modernization we\u0027ve needed to support identity providers (starting with Google). \n\nSSO-first registration flows, this is good.\n\n## What we need to change:\n\n* Add \"require_auth_provider: false\" (default) and preserve legacy registration \u0026 built-in auth.\n\nWhy?  We don\u0027t want to force having a Google (or any other \u0027enteprise\u0027) identity account just to use the robust self-scheduling API.  This is contrary to openness and choice, although that was never the intention of course.  I can also see CI or test/dev really breaking on forced SSO, or users who utilize mailing list groups / email aliases e.g. cpt-ocp-scale@example.com as they don\u0027t get service accounts. \n\nWe should instead (if an identity provider is setup) to make it appealing to use those newer workflows but not break existing API usage that\u0027s grown substantially over the last few years.\n\n* Fix assignments delete API breakage.  Currently the ability to send the assignment ID in a JSON payload disallows legacy SSM users from terminating their own assignment.\n\nRecommended changes:  https://gist.github.com/sadsfae/8f789408513063143ae3ae63ec9afa2e\n\n\n^^^^^^ Probably best slopsummary of everything but I included slop below as well.\n\n\n\nSOME SLOP\n\nRecommendation: Restore backward compatibility immediately by decorating the function to accept both the old payload route and the new path-variable route.\n\n```\n# src/quads/server/blueprints/assignments.py\n\n@assignment_bp.route(\"/\", methods\u003d[\"DELETE\"])                  # Legacy scripts\n@assignment_bp.route(\"/\u003cint:assignment_id\u003e/\", methods\u003d[\"DELETE\"]) # New standard\n@check_access([\"admin\"])\ndef delete_assignment(assignment_id\u003dNone) -\u003e Response:\n    # Fallback for legacy scripts sending JSON payloads\n    if assignment_id is None:\n        data \u003d request.get_json(silent\u003dTrue) or {}\n        assignment_id \u003d data.get(\"id\")\n        \n        if not assignment_id:\n            return make_response(jsonify({\"error\": \"Missing argument: id\"}), 400)\n\n    try:\n        AssignmentDao.delete_assignment(int(assignment_id))\n    except EntryNotFound:\n        return make_response(jsonify({\"error\": \"There was no such entry found\"}), 400)\n\n    return Response(status\u003d204)\n```\n\nSOME SLOP BELOW\n\nPatch already lays the database groundwork for bob@example.com to use both methods seamlessly. In migrations/versions/cf4d6cca178b_add_oauth_fields_to_user.py, the patch adds a google_id and makes the password column nullable\u003dTrue.\n\nBecause the email address is your source of truth, identity mapping is just an upsert during the SSO callback. Recommendation: In the SSO callback logic (e.g., inside get_or_create_oauth_user), look up the user by email first. If legacy Bob logs in via SSO, just attach the Google ID to his existing account.\n\n```\ndef get_or_create_oauth_user(oauth_profile):\n    email \u003d oauth_profile.get(\"email\")\n    google_id \u003d oauth_profile.get(\"id\")\n    \n    # 1. Look up existing legacy user by email\n    user \u003d db.session.query(User).filter(User.email \u003d\u003d email).first()\n    \n    if user:\n        # 2. Map the identities by saving the google_id to the legacy user\n        if not user.google_id:\n            user.google_id \u003d google_id\n            db.session.commit()\n    else:\n        # 3. Create a brand new SSO-only user (no password needed)\n        user \u003d User(email\u003demail, google_id\u003dgoogle_id, password\u003dNone)\n        db.session.add(user)\n        db.session.commit()\n        \n    return user\n```\n\n\nBecause the patch does not actually remove the backend API endpoints for legacy registration or login, that entire **Register -\u003e Login -\u003e Token** loop will survive unharmed. The database migration simply makes the `password` column nullable to support SSO-only users; it doesn\u0027t drop the column.\n\nThis means your script-based users can keep doing exactly what they are doing, and open-source adopters can run QUADS completely independently of Google SSO.\n\nTo finalize this patch and ensure it is safe to merge, here is the comprehensive checklist of the modifications you need to add to it:\n\n### 1. Implement the `require_auth_provider` Toggle\n\nAdd the default value to `conf/selfservice.yml` to preserve out-of-the-box open-source functionality.\n\n```yaml\n# conf/selfservice.yml\nrequire_auth_provider: false\n\n```\n\n### 2. Gate the Legacy Registration \u0026 Login Endpoints\n\nSince the routes still exist, you just need to wrap them so they respect the new configuration. In the files where your API registration and login endpoints live (likely `users.py` or `auth.py` in the API blueprints), add a quick check at the top of the functions:\n\n```python\nfrom flask import current_app, make_response, jsonify\n\n# Inside your API Login and API Register routes:\nif current_app.config.get(\"REQUIRE_AUTH_PROVIDER\", False):\n    return make_response(\n        jsonify({\"error\": \"Legacy API authentication is disabled. Please use SSO.\"}), \n        403\n    )\n\n```\n\n### 3. Gate the Legacy Token Middleware\n\nSimilarly, ensure that if an admin *does* flip the switch to `true` in the future, old legacy tokens are actively rejected during API calls.\n\n```python\n# src/quads/server/blueprints/__init__.py\n                        else:\n                            if current_app.config.get(\"REQUIRE_AUTH_PROVIDER\", False):\n                                return Response(\n                                    json.dumps({\"error\": \"Legacy token auth is disabled. Use an API Token.\"}), \n                                    status\u003d401\n                                )\n                            \n                            # Proceed with normal legacy JWT validation\n                            username \u003d User.decode_auth_token(token_value)\n\n```\n\n### 4. Implement Identity Mapping for Co-existence\n\nTo allow `bob@example.com` to use both his legacy script tokens and log into the web UI via SSO, map the SSO identity to his existing email during the SSO callback.\n\n```python\ndef get_or_create_oauth_user(oauth_profile):\n    email \u003d oauth_profile.get(\"email\")\n    google_id \u003d oauth_profile.get(\"id\")\n    \n    # Look up legacy user by email first\n    user \u003d db.session.query(User).filter(User.email \u003d\u003d email).first()\n    \n    if user:\n        if not user.google_id:\n            user.google_id \u003d google_id\n            db.session.commit()\n    else:\n        user \u003d User(email\u003demail, google_id\u003dgoogle_id, password\u003dNone)\n        db.session.add(user)\n        db.session.commit()\n        \n    return user\n\n```\n\n### 5. Fix the Assignments `DELETE` Breakage\n\nRevert the breaking change to the assignments API by decorating the endpoint to accept both the legacy JSON payload and the new path variable.\n\n```python\n# src/quads/server/blueprints/assignments.py\n@assignment_bp.route(\"/\", methods\u003d[\"DELETE\"])                  # Keeps existing scripts alive\n@assignment_bp.route(\"/\u003cint:assignment_id\u003e/\", methods\u003d[\"DELETE\"]) # Supports the new standard\n@check_access([\"admin\"])\ndef delete_assignment(assignment_id\u003dNone) -\u003e Response:\n    if assignment_id is None:\n        data \u003d request.get_json(silent\u003dTrue) or {}\n        assignment_id \u003d data.get(\"id\")\n        \n        if not assignment_id:\n            return make_response(jsonify({\"error\": \"Missing argument: id\"}), 400)\n\n    try:\n        AssignmentDao.delete_assignment(int(assignment_id))\n    except EntryNotFound:\n        return make_response(jsonify({\"error\": \"There was no such entry found\"}), 400)\n\n    return Response(status\u003d204)\n\n```\n\nWith these 5 additions, the patch will safely introduce permanent tokens and SSO capabilities without violating the spirit of open-source choice or breaking a single existing self-scheduling workflow.\n\n\nhttps://gist.github.com/sadsfae/8f789408513063143ae3ae63ec9afa2e",
